WebKit: the UI process tells the client how many wheel events the page has not answered yet.

LazyWeb shows its picture ahead of the page while the page is busy (the window answers the wheel at once, the
page after its running script ends: 300 to 900 ms while a heavy page loads). It used to guess when to give the
picture back (500 ms after the last notch, 900 ms without a move), and a page busier than that made the picture
jump back and then forward again (issue #940). WKPageSetWheelDoneCallback says, after every wheel event is
queued, sent, dropped or answered, how many the page's process has not answered (the coalescer's queue and the
coalesced events in flight, each counting every event it merged), and 0 when the process ended. The page's
process says it scrolled (PageDidScroll, WKPageSetScrollOffsetCallback) before it answers the event that
scrolled it, on the same connection, so at each call the offset last told is where the answered events left the
page: the window keeps its picture ahead while events are unanswered and gives back exactly what the page did not
do (the end of the page, a box that scrolled instead, a handler that cancelled the wheel).

